<p>&#8220;State, provincial, and national policymakers in Canada, the UK, and US have pursued controversial proposals regulating access to gender affirming care, public bathrooms, and competitive sports&#8230; Many trans and nonbinary philosophers find themselves forced to make difficult decisions about where to apply for graduate school or jobs, without reliable information about conditions on the ground.&#8221;<span id="more-58127"></span></p>
<p>Obtaining information about that is one of the aims of the &#8220;Academic Philosophy and Trans Identity Survey.&#8221;</p>
<p>The research team behind the survey, <a href="https://philosophy.ucmerced.edu/content/dan-hicks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dan Hicks</a> (University of California, Merced, USA), <a href="https://www.catherinestinson.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Catherine Stinson</a> (Queen’s University, ON, Canada), <a href="https://www.jingyiwu.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jingyi Wu</a> (London School of Economics, UK), <a href="https://sites.google.com/view/ray-pedersen/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ray Pedersen</a> (University of Oxford, UK), and <a href="https://www.queensu.ca/philosophy/people/sofie-vlaad" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sofie Vlaad</a> (Queen’s University, ON, Canada) discuss the survey, which you may have received an invitation to complete, in the following guest post.</p>
<hr />
<h2><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="wp-image-58128 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted.png" alt="" width="585" height="400" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted.png 1377w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted-300x205.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted-1024x700.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted-768x525.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/phi-dotted-400x273.png 400w" sizes="(max-width: 585px) 100vw, 585px" /></h2>
<h2 style="text-align: center;">Academic Philosophy and Trans Identity Survey<br />
<em><strong>by Dan Hicks, Catherine Stinson, Jingyi Wu, Ray Pedersen, &amp; Sofie Vlaad</strong></em></h2>
<p>Many readers of this blog have recently received an invitation to participate in the <strong>Academic Philosophy and Trans Identity Survey</strong>. Because we all receive many requests to participate in surveys, as the creators of this survey we wanted to write this blog post to explain our motivations and encourage participation.</p>
<p>Debates over trans and nonbinary identities and the metaphysics of gender are less heated today in Anglophone philosophy than they were around 2019. But these issues have become even more socially salient and politically polarizing. State, provincial, and national policymakers in Canada, the UK, and US have pursued controversial proposals regulating access to gender affirming care, public bathrooms, and competitive sports. Philosophers have provided expert testimony and formal comments supporting or challenging definitions of gender. Many trans and nonbinary philosophers find themselves forced to make difficult decisions about where to apply for graduate school or jobs, without reliable information about conditions on the ground.</p>
<p>We developed the Academic Philosophy and Trans Identity research survey to improve the profession’s understanding in three areas:</p>
<ol>
<li>The prevalence of trans and nonbinary individuals among academic philosophers;</li>
<li>The distribution of philosophical views on the metaphysics of gender; and</li>
<li>The local climate for trans and nonbinary individuals at institutions of higher education.</li>
</ol>
<p>After your responses are collected and analyzed, we plan to share our findings with a blog post here on <em>Daily Nous</em>, a public data dashboard, and a more traditional journal article. We hope the philosophical views information will be of broad interest, and that the climate data will be useful for job seekers, grad school applicants, and departments themselves.</p>
<p>You might be wondering how we got your email address; or, why you didn’t receive an invitation despite having an established position in a philosophy department. For one thing, due to the complex logistics of multinational ethics review, we confined our scope to just the US, Canada, and the UK. It simply wasn’t feasible for our small team to also include Australia, New Zealand, and of course the many European and Latin American countries where scholars publish regularly in Anglophone philosophy.</p>
<p>While we initially approached professional organizations for philosophers in our three focal countries&#8212;the idea being that they could send the survey out to their members&#8212;the responses were uneven, and we were worried about adequate coverage. Instead, we ended up retrieving names and email addresses for articles published in the last 5 years in <a href="https://philpapers.org/pubs.pl?listId=1&amp;journals=1">the 71 most popular philosophy journals on PhilPapers</a>. We then used the email domain (e.g., @ucmerced.edu) to identify institutional affiliations, and filtered down to institutions in the UK, US, and Canada. We also included gmail.com addresses to cover recent graduates and academics in term-limited positions. While this approach was efficient for a small team doing a large project without any funding, it did mean we weren’t able to include colleagues who have not published recently in those journals. If you were not contacted directly, you may participate in the survey using <a href="https://ucmerced.az1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_9MITXJ4UEFDL2jI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">this open participation link</a>.</p>
<p>We emphasize that your email address will not be linked to your responses. Indeed, because the members of the core research team are all philosophers, to protect confidentiality we are working with two biostatisticians at UC Merced&#8212;a professor and one of his graduate students&#8212;who will be the only people who handle the raw data from Qualtrics. Most of our research team will not even have access to the data after it’s cleaned and de-identified.</p>
<p>If you have any questions or concerns before filling in the survey, please contact the project lead at <a href="mailto:dhicks4@ucmerced.edu">dhicks4@ucmerced.edu</a>.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/08/20/academic-philosophy-and-trans-identity-survey-guest-post/">Academic Philosophy and Trans Identity Survey (guest post)</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>